[[incremental-reindex]]
Incremental reindex

An incremental index keeps itself current by re-parsing only the files that changed
since the last run, rather than rebuilding the whole corpus every time. On each read,
the tool compares the modification time of every source file against what it recorded
previously and re-parses just the stale ones before answering. The cost scales with
the size of the edit, not the size of the repository.

This fresh-by-construction model means there is no separate build step to remember and
no window in which the answer reflects an out-of-date snapshot. It mirrors how an
incremental compiler only rebuilds the translation units whose inputs moved. A full
rebuild is reserved for large external events — cloning a new repository or a big
merge — where re-parsing everything is genuinely warranted.
